Done, built with TDD — every test was written first and watched fail before any implementation.

**`src/utils.js`** — added alongside the existing `greet`:

```js
function isValidEmail(email) {
  if (typeof email !== 'string') return false;

  const at = email.indexOf('@');
  // at === 0 means nothing precedes the @; -1 means no @ at all.
  if (at < 1) return false;
  return email.slice(at + 1).includes('.');
}
```

**`test/utils.test.js`** — 5 tests using Node's built-in `node:test`, so no dependencies were added to what was a zero-dependency project. **`package.json`** gained `"test": "node --test test/"`.

Cycles run:
1. Four tests for your three rules — all failed with `isValidEmail is not a function`, then passed against the implementation above.
2. One more test for `undefined` input — failed with `Cannot read properties of undefined`, then passed after the `typeof` guard.

Final run: 5 passed, 0 failed, no warnings.

One thing I decided rather than asked: your spec says "return true/false", and a non-string input crashed instead of returning a boolean, so I added the type guard. If you'd rather it throw on bad input, that's a one-line change plus a test flip.

The dot check looks only at the domain (after the `@`), so `user.name@example` is correctly rejected. This is deliberately a structural check, not RFC 5322 validation — `user@.com` and `a@b.` both pass, since your rules don't say the dot must have characters around it. Say the word if you want those tightened.
